-- Passage wg10-narrative-writing-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6b9a61da-4ab9-5ad9-ac8e-12baaac7183d', 'wg10-narrative-writing-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bridge Labor, 1931$q$, $q$(1) I went to the Calloway County archive to find one fact for a history project, and I left with a story I had not been looking for. (2) My assignment was to learn when the old Pine Street bridge had been built. (3) The archivist, Ms. Delphine Arceneaux, brought me a stack of town ledgers bound in cracked brown leather. (4) The pages were old.

(5) For an hour I turned pages filled with slanted handwriting about road repairs and lumber prices. (6) Meanwhile, at home, my grandfather was sorting his boxes for a move to a smaller apartment, and he had texted me a photo of a bridge with three young men standing on it. (7) I did not think the two things were connected. (8) Then, on a page dated June 1931, I found a list of workers paid for "bridge labor." (9) The third name was Ezekiel Banda, my grandfather's father.

(10) I asked Ms. Arceneaux if I could photograph the page. (11) "Of course," she said, leaning over my shoulder. (12) "You'd be surprised how often people find their own families in here." (13) My hands were not quite steady as I lined up the shot. (14) Many county archives now scan their records so that people can search them online. (15) I sent the picture to my grandfather with a single question mark.

(16) His reply came before I reached the bus stop: "That's him in my photo. Second from the left." (17) I stood on the curb and zoomed in on the young man with rolled sleeves and a crooked grin. (18) The bridge was built in 1931.$q$, 'draft')
