-- Passage wg10-narrative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('60cc69e9-9e03-5e89-a4bc-92bc62f2dd71', 'wg10-narrative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Bread by Lantern$q$, $q$(1) The power went out at our family bakery at four in the morning, two hours before our first customers and five hours before my chemistry final. (2) I had planned to study flashcards at the counter while my mother, Elena Sanchez, shaped the dough. (3) Instead, the ovens went silent and the mixers ground to a stop. (4) In the sudden dark, the only sound was the rain drumming on the front window.

(5) My mother lit two camping lanterns and studied the racks of rising loaves. (6) "If these sit too long, they'll overproof and collapse," she said. (7) "We have maybe an hour." (8) She called our neighbor, Mr. Yamada, who owned the pizza shop across the street and had a gas oven. (9) Within ten minutes he was standing in our doorway with an umbrella and a key.

(10) We carried trays across the street in the rain, one at a time, while the sky turned from black to dark blue. (11) Between trips, I mumbled the terms from my flashcards: molar mass, limiting reagent, percent yield. (12) Mr. Yamada's oven was smaller than ours, so we baked in four batches. (13) The kitchen filled with the warm, toasty smell of crust, and the rain hissed on the hot window glass.

(14) At six, the first customer arrived to find the bread on the shelves, a little uneven but golden. (15) At seven, the lights flickered back on. (16) At eight, I was sitting in room 214 with a pencil, still smelling faintly of yeast. (17) The third question asked about limiting reagents, and I almost laughed. (18) That was my morning.$q$, 'draft')
