insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2b3a5f42-9f6f-50f7-832f-557b0405022e', 'wg10-paragraph-transitions-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cooler Cities from the Top Down$q$, $q$(1) On a summer afternoon, a dark asphalt roof can become far hotter than the air around it, sometimes hot enough to burn a bare hand. (2) Dark surfaces absorb most of the sunlight that strikes them and convert that energy into heat. (3) ____, the buildings beneath them warm up, and their air conditioners must run longer to keep the rooms comfortable. (4) When thousands of roofs in a city behave this way, they help create what scientists call an urban heat island, a zone that stays noticeably warmer than the surrounding countryside.

(5) One response to this problem is the cool roof, a roof covered with a light-colored or specially coated material that reflects much of the incoming sunlight. (6) ____ a dark roof might reflect only a small fraction of the light that reaches it, a bright white coating can send most of that energy back toward the sky. (7) The difference shows up indoors, where top-floor rooms stay cooler and electricity bills often drop during the hottest months. (8) Some older buildings also have decorative stone carvings above their front doors.

(9) Cool roofs are not the right choice for every place, however. (10) In regions with long, cold winters, a building may actually benefit from the heat a dark roof absorbs, so a reflective roof could raise heating costs. (11) White coatings also need regular cleaning, because dust and grime gradually reduce how much light they reflect. (12) ____, city planners in hot climates increasingly see cool roofs as one of the simplest ways to make neighborhoods more livable.$q$, 'draft')
