insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8dee2241-7eb3-5888-86fa-aa77b63c240f', 'wg10-paragraph-transitions-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Eight Rowers, One Rhythm$q$, $q$(1) To a spectator on the riverbank, a racing shell with eight rowers seems to glide forward with almost no effort. (2) In reality, that smooth motion depends on a level of coordination that takes crews months to develop. (3) Every rower must place the oar blade in the water at exactly the same instant, pull with matching force, and lift the blade out together. (4) If even one rower is a fraction of a second early or late, the boat rocks to one side and loses speed.

(5) Because rowers sit facing backward, toward the stern of the boat, they cannot see where they are going. (6) ____, the crew relies on the coxswain, a small team member who sits at the stern facing forward. (7) The coxswain steers the boat, calls out the rhythm of the strokes, and tells the crew when to push harder. (8) ____, a good coxswain also watches the other boats and decides when the crew should try to pass a rival. (9) This is why the coxswain is often described as a coach who travels inside the boat.

(10) Crews build their timing through hours of drills on the water and on rowing machines in the gym. (11) In one common drill, only half the crew pulls while the others hold their oars flat to keep the boat balanced. (12) The rowing season usually ends with a championship race in late spring. (13) Exercises like this train each athlete to feel the rhythm of the boat rather than simply counting strokes.$q$, 'draft')
