   $q$Read the sentences: "The museum reopened in June. After a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden. Attendance doubled." Which revision fixes the fragment?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The museum reopened in June; after a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden. Attendance doubled.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-phrase"},{"text":"The museum reopened in June after a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden. Attendance doubled.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The museum reopened in June. After a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden; attendance doubled.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-after-phrase"},{"text":"The museum reopened in June, after a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden, attendance doubled.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice-created"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The museum reopened in June after a two-year renovation that added a rooftop garden. Attendance doubled.$q$, $q$The prepositional phrase "after a two-year renovation" belongs with the clause about the museum reopening, and attaching it there leaves two complete sentences. Joining everything with commas fuses two independent clauses with only a comma, which is a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
