   $q$Which sentence, added after sentence 5, best explains how the quotation in sentence 5 supports the writer's analysis?$q$, $q$[{"text":"This quotation appears near the beginning of the story, before the foggy morning practice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"location-not-analysis"},{"text":"Glaring at a teammate is rude, and a good athlete should always show respect during practice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"judgment-not-analysis"},{"text":"Blaming Tessa shows that, at this point, Mei cannot yet see that the real problem is her own refusal to match the crew.","is_correct":true},{"text":"This shows that Tessa is the weakest rower on the team and is the true cause of the uneven strokes.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Blaming Tessa shows that, at this point, Mei cannot yet see that the real problem is her own refusal to match the crew.$q$, $q$The quotation matters because it shows Mei blaming someone else before she understands the rhythm problem, which sets up her later change. The choice blaming Tessa accepts Mei's view, even though the coach says Mei's own power is "fighting the boat."$q$, 'draft', '208778e9-eda2-5ea7-bd39-41e7bfa0a4e1')
