-- Passage wg10-writing-with-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ca6d2cdb-7b02-594e-b9ce-ffad53a7f469', 'wg10-writing-with-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Young Hands on the Telegraph$q$, $q$(1) In "Messages on the Wire," historian Kofi Asante describes the telegraph office that opened in the river town of Larkfield in 1872. (2) Asante's article is about a telegraph office in a small town. (3) He explains that the office hired several operators who were only fifteen or sixteen years old, because young people learned the clicking code faster than most adults did. (4) According to Asante, the station manager wrote in his logbook that his youngest operator "could take down a message faster than I could read it aloud." (5) This record shows that the town relied on the skill of teenagers to keep its news and business moving.

(6) Asante gives more evidence of how much responsibility these operators carried. (7) During the spring flood of 1874, a sixteen-year-old operator named Hattie Brennan stayed at her station for two days, sending warnings to farms downstream. (8) ____ her messages, Asante reports, every family on the lower river reached high ground before the water rose. (9) The telegraph itself had been invented several decades before the Larkfield office opened. (10) Her story suggests that the young operators were not just helpers but people whose quick work protected the whole community.

(11) That is what I learned about the telegraph.$q$, 'draft')
