  ('f35ea005-988d-56f2-9843-422ac6931efa', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-writing-with-evidence'), null, 'hard',
   $q$A classmate suggests replacing sentence 7 with "The operators worked very hard during emergencies." Why is the original sentence 7 the stronger choice?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It includes a direct quotation from Hattie Brennan describing how she felt during the flood.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-passage"},{"text":"It gives a specific event with a name, a date and an outcome, which proves the point better than a general statement.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is longer, and longer sentences always make an essay sound more convincing to readers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"length-equals-strength"},{"text":"It tells the reader how the telegraph machine itself worked, which explains the operators' skill.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-passage"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It gives a specific event with a name, a date and an outcome, which proves the point better than a general statement.$q$, $q$Specific, documented details make evidence convincing, and sentence 7 names who acted, when and what happened. Sentence 7 contains no quotation from Brennan, so the choice claiming it does misreads the draft.$q$, 'draft', 'ca6d2cdb-7b02-594e-b9ce-ffad53a7f469')
