-- Passage wg10-writing-with-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('42e9e6fb-6d67-5df5-9790-4d353b6e41c8', 'wg10-writing-with-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: The Silent Distraction$q$, $q$(1) In her report "The Buzz in the Background," psychologist Hana Kobayashi describes an experiment with 240 students at Ridgeview High School. (2) Many teenagers own phones and use them every day. (3) Each student read the same science article and then answered questions about it, but some kept their phones face down on the desk while others left them in a locker down the hall. (4) Kobayashi reports that the students whose phones were in lockers scored an average of 14 percent higher on the questions. (5) Because the phones on the desks were silent and never lit up, this result suggests that simply having a phone in sight pulls some attention away from reading.

(6) Kobayashi's interviews with the students point in the same direction. (7) Several students in the desk group admitted that they "kept wondering if something had come in," even though they knew their phones were set to silent. (8) ____, students in the locker group described the article as easy to follow, although both groups read identical text. (9) One student in the study said she prefers reading paper books to reading on a screen. (10) Taken together, the scores and the students' own words show that a phone does not have to ring to be a distraction.

(11) So phones are bad.$q$, 'draft')
