        '9-12', $q$Draft: Rain on the Rooftops$q$, $q$(1) In "Every Drop on the Roof," engineer Farah Haddad describes how the dry hill town of Sela Verde began collecting rainwater from its rooftops. (2) Haddad explains that the town receives most of its yearly rain in only six weeks, and that this water once ran down the streets and into a dry riverbed. (3) In 2018, residents installed gutters and storage tanks on 400 homes. (4) ____, Haddad reports, the tanks held enough water to supply each household's garden for most of the dry season.

(5) Haddad gives several reasons the project worked. (6) The tanks were built by local workers using parts that could be repaired in town, so broken pipes did not have to wait for shipments from the city. (7) In addition, the town held workshops where families learned to clean their gutters before the rainy season. (8) Haddad writes that the workshops mattered because "a tank fed by a dirty roof soon becomes a tank no one wants to use." (9) Haddad has written two other articles about water in dry regions.

(10) Clearly, Haddad's article shows that rooftop tanks would work in every town in the world.$q$, 'draft')
