        '9-12', $q$Open Textbooks for Ridgeway$q$, $q$(1) Each fall, Ridgeway High School spends about $180,000 replacing worn or outdated printed textbooks. (2) The district should adopt openly licensed digital textbooks for its core science and history courses. (3) Openly licensed texts are written by educators and released for free use, so the district would pay only for review and for optional paper copies. (4) A pilot in the neighboring Alder Valley district found that students using open texts scored about the same on end-of-course exams as students using purchased books. (5) Digital texts can also be updated each year, which matters in fields like biology, where findings change quickly. (6) Printed books are also heavy.

(7) Opponents argue that digital texts disadvantage students who lack reliable devices or internet access at home. (8) Ridgeway already lends a laptop to every student, but home internet remains a real gap for roughly one in eight families. (9) The district could address this gap by letting students download chapters at school for offline reading and by printing low-cost copies for any family that requests one.

(10) Some teachers also say they prefer the familiar layout of the books they have used for years. (11) Familiarity has value, but teachers in the Alder Valley pilot reported adjusting to the new texts within a single semester.

(12) Open digital textbooks would save money, stay current and, with sensible supports, serve every student fairly. (13) The board should approve them for next fall.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
