   $q$A student wrote about a bridge inspection. Which sentence is punctuated correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The bridge inspection took longer than expected, however, the road reopened on schedule.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"The bridge inspection took longer than expected; however, the road reopened on schedule.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The bridge inspection took longer than expected: however the road reopened on schedule.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-before-transition"},{"text":"The bridge inspection took longer than expected however; the road reopened on schedule.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-after-transition"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The bridge inspection took longer than expected; however, the road reopened on schedule.$q$, $q$When "however" connects two independent clauses, a semicolon comes before it and a comma follows it. Using commas on both sides of "however" leaves two independent clauses joined only by a comma, which is a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
