   $q$The chemistry teacher wants to say that ONLY the students who finished early helped clean the equipment. Which sentence is punctuated to show that meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The students, who finished the lab early helped clean the equipment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"missing-closing-comma"},{"text":"The students who finished the lab early helped clean the equipment.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The students who finished the lab early, helped clean the equipment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-subject-verb"},{"text":"The students, who finished the lab early, helped clean the equipment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"restrictive-clause-set-off"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The students who finished the lab early helped clean the equipment.$q$, $q$Without commas, "who finished the lab early" is essential: it identifies which students helped. Setting it off with commas would make it extra information and suggest that all of the students finished early and helped.$q$, 'draft', null)
