   $q$Which sentence uses punctuation correctly to introduce an explanation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The master glassblower gave: the apprentices one piece of advice, never rush the cooling process.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-verb"},{"text":"The master glassblower gave the apprentices one piece of advice: never rush the cooling process.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The master glassblower gave the apprentices one: piece of advice never rush the cooling process.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-misplaced"},{"text":"The master glassblower gave the apprentices one piece of advice, never rush the cooling process.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The master glassblower gave the apprentices one piece of advice: never rush the cooling process.$q$, $q$A colon after a complete clause can introduce the specific item the clause points to, here the piece of advice. A colon placed after "one" cuts the phrase "one piece of advice" in two before the clause is complete.$q$, 'draft', null)
