   $q$Which sentence about the water samples is punctuated correctly?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The first water samples were contaminated, the second set, however, produced reliable data.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"The first water samples were contaminated; the second set, however, produced reliable data.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The first water samples were contaminated; however the second set, produced reliable data.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-between-subject-verb"},{"text":"The first water samples were contaminated; the second set; however, produced reliable data.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-for-comma"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The first water samples were contaminated; the second set, however, produced reliable data.$q$, $q$A semicolon joins the two independent clauses, and "however" sits in the middle of the second clause, so it is set off by commas on both sides. Commas around "however" cannot also join the two clauses, so the version with only commas, "contaminated, the second set, however," is a comma splice.$q$, 'draft', null)
