        '9-12', $q$Cool Roofs and the Urban Heat Island$q$, $q$(1) On a summer afternoon in the city of Marrowfield, the air above downtown can be several degrees warmer than the air over the farm fields just outside town. (2) Scientists call this pattern an urban heat island, a zone where buildings, pavement and rooftops absorb sunlight during the day and release it as heat long after sunset. (3) Dark surfaces are the main culprits because they have low albedo, meaning they reflect only a small fraction of the sunlight that strikes them.

(4) One response is the cool roof, a roof coated or built with pale, reflective materials. (5) A fresh white coating can reflect more than three quarters of incoming sunlight, while a traditional black tar roof may reflect less than a tenth. (6) In 2023, a team from the Halvorsen Climate Lab coated the roofs of forty warehouses in Marrowfield's east district and measured indoor temperatures for two summers. (7) Buildings with treated roofs averaged 4 degrees cooler indoors on the hottest days, and their electricity use for air conditioning fell by roughly 15 percent. (8) My uncle painted his garage roof white last year, and he said it looked very bright.

(9) Cool roofs are not a perfect solution. (10) In cities with long, cold winters, a reflective roof can slightly raise heating costs, since it also rejects warming sunlight in January. (11) Coatings also lose some reflectivity as dust and soot accumulate, so they must be cleaned or renewed every several years.

(12) Overall, cool roofs are an interesting topic.$q$, 'draft')
