insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c5fcfabc-5913-5c0a-96b0-998768ede1b4', 'wg11-informative-writing-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Harvesting Water from Fog$q$, $q$(1) Along the dry coast near the village of Punta Cardo, rain may fall only a few days each year, yet thick fog rolls in from the ocean most mornings. (2) For generations, residents hauled drinking water from wells several kilometers inland. (3) Since 2019, however, a row of fog collectors on the ridge above the village has supplied a growing share of the community's water.

(4) A fog collector is a tall frame strung with fine mesh netting that stands perpendicular to the prevailing wind. (5) Fog consists of countless droplets so tiny that they float rather than fall. (6) When the wind pushes fog through the mesh, droplets strike the fibers, merge into larger drops and trickle down into a gutter. (7) Pipes then carry the water downhill to a storage tank beside the village school.

(8) The amount of water collected depends on a bunch of things. (9) Denser fog and steadier wind produce more water, and the design of the mesh matters as well. (10) Engineer Lucia Ferreyra, who helped plan the Punta Cardo system, tested three mesh patterns and found that a double layer of knitted fiber captured nearly twice as much water as the single layer used in earlier projects. (11) On a typical foggy morning, each large panel now yields about 200 liters.

(12) Fog collectors are really cool and everybody should build them.$q$, 'draft')
