-- Passage wg11-informative-writing-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1e7810df-a7a7-5db4-a5eb-fa46657bdc9c', 'wg11-informative-writing-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Mail Beneath the Streets$q$, $q$(1) Long before electronic messages, several large cities moved letters beneath their streets using compressed air. (2) Pneumatic tube mail, as the system was called, sent sealed metal canisters through underground pipes between post offices. (3) The city of Brennholt opened its network in 1887 with just four stations, but by 1910 its pipes stretched more than 120 kilometers.

(4) The principle was simple. (5) Steam-powered pumps at central stations pushed air into the tubes behind each canister or drew air out ahead of it, creating a pressure difference that propelled the canister forward. (6) Canisters traveled at roughly 35 kilometers per hour, far faster than a horse-drawn mail wagon in crowded traffic. (7) A telegram that once took an hour to deliver across town could arrive in under ten minutes. (8) Brennholt was also known for the large clock tower in its central square.

(9) The system declined after the 1950s. (10) Telephones made urgent written messages less necessary, and maintaining aging pipes became expensive. (11) Brennholt closed its last tube line in 1963, and most of the old pipes were sealed and left beneath the pavement.$q$, 'draft')
