-- Passage wg11-informative-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c8218ee2-5998-5701-b8f6-f32e90dc055b', 'wg11-informative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Running with a Pacer$q$, $q$(1) In many large marathons, runners can spot volunteers carrying small flags marked with a finishing time, such as three and a half or four hours. (2) These volunteers are pacers, experienced runners who agree to cover the course at a precise, steady speed so that others can follow them toward a target time.

(3) Pacing matters because of how the body uses fuel. (4) Muscles store a limited supply of glycogen, a form of carbohydrate that powers hard effort. (5) Runners who start too fast burn through glycogen early and often slow dramatically in the final miles, a collapse that athletes call "hitting the wall." (6) A steady pace spreads that limited supply across the entire race.

(7) Research supports the value of even pacing. (8) A 2021 analysis of 9,000 finishers at the Lakeport City Marathon found that runners whose second-half times were within 3 percent of their first-half times were far less likely to slow severely after mile 20. (9) Pacers make even pacing easier by removing the need to check a watch constantly. (10) Pacers also have to wear brightly colored shirts.

(11) Some coaches warn about pacers. (12) Runners in a crowded pace group may waste energy weaving around others, and a group pace may not match an individual runner's fitness on that day.

(13) In conclusion, pacers are people who run marathons.$q$, 'draft')
