-- Passage wg11-informative-writing-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f2f16490-877e-5795-bcc8-8881e3af32e0', 'wg11-informative-writing-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving Early Film$q$, $q$(1) In the first half of the twentieth century, most motion pictures were printed on cellulose nitrate film, a flexible plastic base coated with a light-sensitive layer. (2) Nitrate produced sharp, luminous images, but it carried a serious flaw: as it ages, it breaks down chemically. (3) Archivists today work to slow that decay and to copy fragile films before they are lost.

(4) Decomposition happens in stages. (5) First, the image fades and the base turns amber. (6) Next, the film becomes sticky and gives off a sharp odor. (7) Eventually, the reel softens into brown powder, and the images on it cannot be recovered.

(8) Cold storage is the archivist's main defense. (9) At the Corvane Film Archive, nitrate reels are kept in vaults held at 4 degrees Celsius and 35 percent relative humidity. (10) According to the archive's conservator, Amara Nwosu, every 5 degree drop in temperature roughly doubles the expected life of a reel. (11) I think old movies are better than new ones anyway.

(12) Storage alone cannot save every film, so archives also make copies. (13) Technicians scan each frame at high resolution, creating digital files that can be studied and screened without handling the original.

(14) That is how film archives work.$q$, 'draft')
