-- Passage wg11-narrative-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d008e018-c6f1-5280-96c4-51d8ae68e90a', 'wg11-narrative-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Conductor's Watch$q$, $q$(1) For our history class's oral history project, each of us had to interview someone who had lived in Carver Falls for at least fifty years. (2) I chose Mr. Benedict Asante, who had worked as a conductor on the regional passenger train until the line closed in 1989. (3) I expected a short, polite conversation in the lobby of his apartment building. (4) Instead, he invited me upstairs, poured two glasses of lemonade, and set a heavy brass pocket watch on the table between us.

(5) "This watch kept the whole line on time," he said. (6) "If I was a minute late, three hundred people were a minute late." (7) He described the morning rush, the smell of diesel and wet wool coats, and a winter when snowdrifts buried the tracks up to the platform edge. (8) I was writing so fast that my notes became almost unreadable, so I switched on the recorder on my phone.

(9) Meanwhile, my partner Lucia was struggling with an interview across town. (10) Lucia's subject, a retired seamstress, would only answer yes or no. (11) Lucia texted me twice asking what questions I was using.

(12) When I finally stood up to leave, Mr. Asante opened the watch and showed me a tiny engraving inside the lid: a train and the date the line opened. (13) He said his father had given it to him. (14) Then he closed it and pressed it into my hand for a moment before taking it back. (15) "Somebody should remember the times," he said.

(16) I took the bus home.$q$, 'draft')
