-- Passage wg11-narrative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('889c5e8c-d2ae-54ab-bcb8-2938fb757d75', 'wg11-narrative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Gap in the Clouds$q$, $q$(1) The forecast promised clear skies, so on Friday night the astronomy club hauled two telescopes, a cooler of cocoa, and a pile of folding chairs to the top of Harmon Hill. (2) A comet was supposed to be visible low in the western sky for one more week, and after that it would not return for thousands of years. (3) Our club president, Ines, had been talking about it since August.

(4) By nine o'clock, a bank of clouds had rolled in from the west. (5) We waited. (6) Ines kept checking the weather radar on a phone, then looking at the sky as though the clouds could be argued open. (7) Meanwhile, my younger brother, Kofi, who had come along only because our parents insisted, fell asleep in a folding chair under two blankets.

(8) At ten-thirty, half the club packed up and left. (9) I almost went with them. (10) The telescope cost about four hundred dollars. (11) Then, just before eleven, a gap opened in the clouds, ragged at the edges, exactly where the comet should be.

(12) Ines swung the telescope toward the gap. (13) There it was: a smudge of pale green light with a faint tail spreading behind it. (14) Ines stepped back to let me look, and I forgot about the cold.

(15) I shook Kofi awake. (16) He looked through the eyepiece for a long time without saying a word. (17) The comet was visible.$q$, 'draft')
