   $q$The writer wants to add a sentence after sentence 7 that builds the narrator's frustration without breaking the sequence of events. Which sentence does this best?$q$, $q$[{"text":"My grandmother had bought the truck from a neighbor the year before I was born.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"backstory-interrupts"},{"text":"Manual transmissions were far more common in cars sold several decades ago.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-fact"},{"text":"By the eleventh stall, my calf was trembling, and my face burned whenever someone walked out of the feed store.","is_correct":true},{"text":"By the fourth afternoon, I could shift into third gear without any trouble at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"breaks-sequence"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$By the eleventh stall, my calf was trembling, and my face burned whenever someone walked out of the feed store.$q$, $q$The trembling calf and burning face show frustration and embarrassment during the first afternoon, so the order stays intact. The fourth afternoon choice jumps ahead and gives away the resolution in sentence 13.$q$, 'draft', '14f45f66-490f-575b-9b5b-685ec761fe27')
